WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Welcome. In this class, you will choose
where a self-hosted

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:10.000
model should run by starting with the work
and the

3
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.000
consequences of failure, not with a
favorite platform. We will

4
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:20.000
define a service contract, compare
workstation, container, on-premises, edge,

5
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:25.000
cloud, and hybrid shapes, map real failure
domains, assign operating

6
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:30.000
ownership, and select a reversible first
step. Self-hosted does not

7
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.000
automatically mean private, inexpensive,
reliable, or under control. Those are

8
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:40.000
claims that the complete system must
prove.

9
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.938
Begin with a workload contract. Name the
users, their tasks,

10
00:00:45.938 --> 00:00:51.875
the model class, input and output types,
sensitivity, concurrency, arrival

11
00:00:51.875 --> 00:00:57.813
pattern, context size, output length,
latency objective, availability target,

12
00:00:57.813 --> 00:01:03.750
geographic boundary, disconnected
requirement, retention rule, and recovery

13
00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:09.688
objective. Separate an individual
experiment from a shared internal service

14
00:01:09.688 --> 00:01:15.625
and from a production dependency. Those
labels change who can

15
00:01:15.625 --> 00:01:21.563
be affected and how quickly someone must
respond. Then describe

16
00:01:21.563 --> 00:01:27.500
failure behavior. What should happen when
the endpoint is slow,

17
00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:33.438
unavailable, overloaded, wrong, unsafe, or
unable to accept a request?

18
00:01:33.438 --> 00:01:39.375
Can work wait, move to a reviewed
fallback, or stop?

19
00:01:39.375 --> 00:01:45.313
Which data may be retained for recovery,
and which data

20
00:01:45.313 --> 00:01:51.250
must never enter the service? A platform
is suitable only

21
00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:57.188
when the team can operate both the normal
path and

22
00:01:57.188 --> 00:02:03.125
the failure path. Starting with hardware
reverses this logic. It

23
00:02:03.125 --> 00:02:09.063
encourages the team to reshape
requirements around a purchase before

24
00:02:09.063 --> 00:02:15.000
it has defined what success, safety, or
recovery mean.

25
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:20.000
Consider a synthetic documentation
assistant used by six engineers. It

26
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:25.000
receives approved public manuals, produces
draft explanations, has short weekday

27
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:30.000
bursts, and may stop safely when evidence
is missing. It

28
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.000
must not process customer records or
secrets. A five-minute interruption

29
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:40.000
delays work but does not cause an external
action. The

30
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:45.000
first claim to prove is that one exact
model and

31
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:50.000
runtime can meet the quality target on
representative manuals within

32
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:55.000
the available memory. This contract does
not yet justify a

33
00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:00.000
multi-site cluster. Now change one fact:
the endpoint becomes a

34
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.000
production dependency for hundreds of
users with a strict recovery

35
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:10.000
objective. The model may be identical, but
ownership, capacity, availability,

36
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:15.000
telemetry, and recovery evidence must
expand. The deployment shape follows

37
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:20.000
the service contract.

38
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:26.765
Compare each shape against the same
contract. A workstation can

39
00:03:26.765 --> 00:03:33.529
be excellent for individual learning,
offline experiments, and low-concurrency

40
00:03:33.529 --> 00:03:40.294
development. It may also sleep, share a
user's privileges, change

41
00:03:40.294 --> 00:03:47.059
without review, throttle under heat, and
disappear when the user

42
00:03:47.059 --> 00:03:53.824
leaves. A container gives the process a
reproducible package and

43
00:03:53.824 --> 00:04:00.588
an explicit runtime boundary. It does not
automatically add authentication,

44
00:04:00.588 --> 00:04:07.353
strong tenant isolation, resource limits,
backups, or high availability. On-premises

45
00:04:07.353 --> 00:04:14.118
infrastructure can support local data or
connectivity requirements and direct

46
00:04:14.118 --> 00:04:20.882
hardware control. In exchange, the
operator owns capacity, patching, power,

47
00:04:20.882 --> 00:04:27.647
cooling, spares, physical access, and
incident response. Edge equipment can

48
00:04:27.647 --> 00:04:34.412
reduce distance and survive intermittent
upstream connectivity, but power, memory,

49
00:04:34.412 --> 00:04:41.176
update bandwidth, physical security, and
repair are constrained. Cloud compute

50
00:04:41.176 --> 00:04:47.941
can speed provisioning and offer managed
building blocks, yet region,

51
00:04:47.941 --> 00:04:54.706
identity, networking, encryption, quota,
cost, observability, backup, and exit

52
00:04:54.706 --> 00:05:01.471
still require proof. Hybrid designs add
routing, consistency, and failure

53
00:05:01.471 --> 00:05:08.235
choices. None of the labels establishes
privacy, price, performance, or

54
00:05:08.235 --> 00:05:15.000
resilience. Write those as measurable
requirements, then collect evidence.

55
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:20.000
Choose the shape that first felt obvious
for your workload.

56
00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:25.000
Write one benefit you expected and one
property you may

57
00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:30.000
have assumed without evidence, such as
privacy, lower cost, availability,

58
00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:35.000
or easier recovery. Then name the
measurement or test that

59
00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:40.000
would support that property.

60
00:06:55.000 --> 00:07:01.333
Next, map failure domains instead of
counting replicas. List the

61
00:07:01.333 --> 00:07:07.667
process, container, accelerator, host,
storage, network, identity provider,

62
00:07:07.667 --> 00:07:14.000
scheduler, site, region, artifact
registry, and management plane. Decide

63
00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:20.333
which failure should restart one process,
replace one replica, move

64
00:07:20.333 --> 00:07:26.667
work to another node, fail over, shed
load, or stop

65
00:07:26.667 --> 00:07:33.000
safely. Two replicas on one host share the
host, device,

66
00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:39.333
power, and often storage and network. They
may improve process

67
00:07:39.333 --> 00:07:45.667
recovery without surviving host loss. Two
sites can still share

68
00:07:45.667 --> 00:07:52.000
identity, registry, routing, or management
dependencies. An orchestrator can restart

69
00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:58.333
a failed process, but it cannot repair an
incompatible artifact,

70
00:07:58.333 --> 00:08:04.667
restore a lost secret, or prove that
queued work completed.

71
00:08:04.667 --> 00:08:11.000
Test the failure domain you claim. Confirm
that exact artifacts,

72
00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:17.333
configuration, state, secrets, queues,
routing, and telemetry remain recoverable.

73
00:08:17.333 --> 00:08:23.667
Availability language should describe
observed survival and recovery, not

74
00:08:23.667 --> 00:08:30.000
topology alone.

75
00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:36.250
Checkpoint. A team runs three model-server
containers on one physical

76
00:08:36.250 --> 00:08:42.500
workstation and calls the endpoint highly
available. Name two failures

77
00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:48.750
that can remove all three replicas, and
state what evidence

78
00:08:48.750 --> 00:08:55.000
would be required before making a
host-availability claim.

79
00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:15.000
A strong answer names shared host power,
operating system, accelerator,

80
00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:20.000
local storage, network, or physical
damage. All three containers can

81
00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:25.000
disappear together. Process replication is
still useful, but the claim

82
00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:30.000
must stay inside that boundary. Host
availability requires independent capacity

83
00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:35.000
and an observed test that removes the
host, restores routing,

84
00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:40.000
reconstructs the exact approved service,
preserves or reconciles state, and

85
00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:45.000
meets the stated recovery objective. If
your answer only added

86
00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:50.000
more containers, revise it by moving the
recovery target outside

87
00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:55.000
the failed host.

88
00:10:55.000 --> 00:11:01.667
A deployable design also needs named
owners. Assign model and

89
00:11:01.667 --> 00:11:08.333
license review, artifact integrity, host
and accelerator lifecycle, runtime,

90
00:11:08.333 --> 00:11:15.000
identity and access, secrets, network
policy, data handling, evaluation,

91
00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:21.667
capacity, cost, telemetry, vulnerability
response, abuse handling, incident

92
00:11:21.667 --> 00:11:28.333
command, backup, restore, rollback, and
end of life. For each

93
00:11:28.333 --> 00:11:35.000
responsibility, record decision authority,
evidence produced, on-call expectation,

94
00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:41.667
escalation path, and a qualified
substitute. A team name is

95
00:11:41.667 --> 00:11:48.333
not enough if nobody can approve a
rollback or access

96
00:11:48.333 --> 00:11:55.000
the recovery system during an incident.
Managed services shift some

97
00:11:55.000 --> 00:12:01.667
tasks to a provider, but the organization
still owns configuration,

98
00:12:01.667 --> 00:12:08.333
access, workload evidence, and the
decision to accept residual risk.

99
00:12:08.333 --> 00:12:15.000
A technically successful endpoint with no
update owner, no cost

100
00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:21.667
owner, or no restore operator is an
unmanaged liability. Find

101
00:12:21.667 --> 00:12:28.333
those gaps before deployment, when
changing the design is still

102
00:12:28.333 --> 00:12:35.000
inexpensive.

103
00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:41.333
Choose the smallest reversible shape that
can safely prove the

104
00:12:41.333 --> 00:12:47.667
next claim. If the question is whether the
immutable artifact

105
00:12:47.667 --> 00:12:54.000
runs and meets representative quality on
available hardware, an isolated

106
00:12:54.000 --> 00:13:00.333
workstation or bounded container may be
enough. If the question

107
00:13:00.333 --> 00:13:06.667
is whether a shared endpoint meets
capacity and access requirements,

108
00:13:06.667 --> 00:13:13.000
use a controlled service environment with
real identity, load, and

109
00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:19.333
telemetry. Do not build multi-site
orchestration before one endpoint has

110
00:13:19.333 --> 00:13:25.667
a verified contract. Record the chosen and
rejected shapes, assumptions,

111
00:13:25.667 --> 00:13:32.000
evidence, unresolved risks, capacity and
cost envelope, required controls, stop

112
00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:38.333
conditions, migration path, and review
date. Define how to return

113
00:13:38.333 --> 00:13:44.667
to a known safe state. Trigger a new
decision when

114
00:13:44.667 --> 00:13:51.000
users, workload, data class, geography,
model, runtime, demand, objectives,

115
00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:57.333
incident history, or ownership changes.
Reversibility is not reluctance. It

116
00:13:57.333 --> 00:14:03.667
is a design property that lets evidence
change the decision

117
00:14:03.667 --> 00:14:10.000
without trapping people, data, or
operations.

118
00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:15.556
Now create the deployment-shape decision
record. Define one synthetic workload

119
00:14:15.556 --> 00:14:21.111
and score workstation, containerized host,
on-premises service, edge, cloud, and

120
00:14:21.111 --> 00:14:26.667
one hybrid option against identical
criteria. Draw process, host, site,

121
00:14:26.667 --> 00:14:32.222
identity, network, storage, registry, and
management failure domains. Assign every

122
00:14:32.222 --> 00:14:37.778
operating role and flag gaps. Choose a
bounded first shape,

123
00:14:37.778 --> 00:14:43.333
explain rejected alternatives, define stop
and migration triggers, and describe

124
00:14:43.333 --> 00:14:48.889
the return to a known safe state. Your
evidence is

125
00:14:48.889 --> 00:14:54.444
the dated workload contract, comparison
matrix, failure map, ownership matrix,

126
00:14:54.444 --> 00:15:00.000
and reversible decision.

127
00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:05.000
When you are ready, begin the knowledge
check. You will

128
00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:10.000
define the pre-platform service contract,
distinguish what a container does

129
00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:15.000
and does not provide, reason about shared
failure domains, evaluate

130
00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:20.000
locality claims, assign actionable
ownership, and choose a reversible first

131
00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:25.000
shape. Review the transcript or decision
record before submitting. The

132
00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:30.000
assessment begins only when you select
Begin knowledge check.

133
00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:35.000
Keep this rule: define the work, failure,
and owner before

134
00:18:35.000 --> 00:18:40.000
choosing the platform. Select the smallest
reversible shape that proves

135
00:18:40.000 --> 00:18:45.000
the next claim, and never describe
privacy, reliability, cost, or

136
00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:50.000
recovery beyond the evidence you have
tested.
